  "query_preview": "(This evening through Monday)...\nIssued at 301 PM EDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nToday through Wednesday...\n\nExpect mostly quiet weather conditions with surface high pressure \nremaining in control across the region. Large scale subsidence and \ndaytime heating has helped to clear out the stratus deck from \nearlier this morning. Lingering scattered cumulus will gradually \nclear out into the evening. Mostly clear skies, very light winds, \nand damp grounds from recent rainfall on Saturday could promote \nadditional fog development late tonight. Any fog that develop will \nmix out in typical diurnal fashion Tuesday morning. Expect \ntemperatures to gradually warm into the middle of week thanks to \nmodest warm air advection. Highs are generally going to be slightly \nbelow or near normal. \n\nWednesday night onward...\n\nGuidance depicts a longwave trough with multiple embedded shortwaves \ntraversing the region Wednesday night through end of the week. These \nimpulses will promote additional convective chances as deeper \nmoisture surges back into central Indiana. The greatest \nprecipitation chances are on Thursday when the strongest forcing \noverlaps with the deeper moisture surging northward. A frontal \nboundary associated with a weak disturbance will likely stall near \nthe region by Thursday. Additional shortwaves tracking along the \nboundary supports daily precipitation chances through at least \nSaturday.\n\nSome guidance suggest upper ridging builds in late this weekend into \nearly next week providing mostly quiet weather conditions as \nshortwaves aloft remain north of central Indiana. However, some \nmodels differ and continue to suggest shortwaves passing through \naloft. These differences leads to lower confidence in the forecast \nso will keep low chances for precipitation Sunday onward. \n\nExact details remain uncertain at this time due to diverging model \nsolutions, but moderate destabilization at times and enhancement of \nmid-upper level flow from the passing waves could potentially \nsupport isolated severe thunderstorms somewhere near the region. \nUncertainty will remain high until models become better aligned.",
